      <div class="card my-4 px-5 pb-5">
        <div class="card-body">
          <h2 class="font-heading">AI Usage Policy</h2>
          <p>
            This is my personal policy about AI usage. I believe in being honest about where LLMs were used in generating code/content.
            I have used LLMs to brainstorm, validate my code quality, help with the JS and automate writing much of the CSS boiler plate.
            None of the images or text are AI-generated and only a so-called "AI background removal tool" has been used for image editing.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <div class="card my-4 px-5 pb-5">
        <div class="card-body">
          <h2 class="font-heading">Easter Eggs & Cool Things</h2>
          <p>Manually switching between dark/light mode on the home page activates an SVG animation whereby the dude at the desk turns on/off his lamp. This is literally just CSS tied to a named element in the in line SVG activated by the vanilla JS dark mode logic. It's simple but it took hours to do. Some may say this was a waste of time but I say the time you enjoy wasting is not wasted time.</p>
          <p>The steam coming off the cup of tea on the hero image is actually an SVG fractal noise filter; I want to learn more about these, because they are crazy powerful.</p>
          <p>Getting the stick figures to change colour dynamically betweeen themes, required in-lining them and using a CSS variable. This is annoying because updating each SVG now requires manually editing the SVG code instead of just getting a raw extract from Figma. I don't recommend this appraoch.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
          
          
